To write a Fibonacci number function in Rust, you can use a simple recursive approach. Here's an example:

```rust
fn fibonacci(n: u32) -> u32 {
    if n <= 1 {
        return n;
    }
    fibonacci(n - 1) + fibonacci(n - 2)
}
```

This function works for small values of `n`, but it's not efficient for larger values due to repeated calculations. For better performance, consider using memoization or an iterative approach.
